{"title":"Tidewell Foods","sub":"A healthy-snack brand: worth $110M to us?","type":"M&A","industry":"Packaged food","geo":"United States","diff":2,"fmt":"Interviewer-led","time":"25-30 min","skills":"M&A valuation · synergies · deal structure","prompt":"Tidewell is a large US snack maker whose sales of crisps and crackers are flat. It wants to buy Greenleaf, a fast-growing healthy-snack brand, and the founders are asking $110M. Tidewell's CEO asks: should we buy, and is $110M the right price?","clarify":[{"q":"Greenleaf's numbers?","a":"EBITDA (operating profit before depreciation) of $8M. Similar snack brands sell for about 10× EBITDA."},{"q":"Cost savings?","a":"Tidewell could buy ingredients and packaging for Greenleaf more cheaply and share warehouses: about $2M a year."},{"q":"Sales upside?","a":"Putting Greenleaf into Tidewell's 30,000 stores could add $15M of sales a year at a 25% margin. The sales team thinks it's a coin flip whether it fully happens."},{"q":"Integration?","a":"About $6M one-off to combine systems and supply chains."}],"structure":{"root":"Should Tidewell buy Greenleaf at $110M?","branches":[{"head":"Value on its own","items":["EBITDA × market multiple"]},{"head":"Value of synergies","items":["Cost savings (more certain)","Extra sales (less certain)","Minus integration costs"]},{"head":"Price","items":["Max we should pay","Deal structure"]},{"head":"Fit and risk","items":["Brand and culture","Founders staying"]}]},"hypothesis":"Greenleaf on its own is probably worth less than $110M, so the price depends on synergies. Cost savings are fairly certain; extra sales much less so.","questions":[{"q":"What is Greenleaf worth on its own, and what are the synergies worth?","exhibit":{"kind":"chart","svg":"<svg xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\" width=\"383.56pt\" height=\"205.56pt\" viewBox=\"0 0 383.56 205.56\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" version=\"1.1\"><defs><style type=\"text/css\">*{stroke-linejoin: round; stroke-linecap: butt